1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:20,120
As an artist, connection is very important to me.

2
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:25,800
Through my work, I'm trying to articulate that humans are not separate from nature,

3
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:29,600
and that everything is interconnected.

4
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:34,720
I first went to Antarctica almost 10 years ago where I saw my first icebergs.

5
00:00:34,720 --> 00:00:36,820
I was in awe.

6
00:00:36,820 --> 00:00:38,120
My heart beat fast.

7
00:00:38,120 --> 00:00:43,440
My head was dizzy trying to comprehend what it was that stood in front of me.

8
00:00:43,440 --> 00:00:48,800
The icebergs around me were almost 200 feet out of the water, and I could only help but

9
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:56,740
wonder that this was one snowflake on top of another snowflake year after year.

10
00:00:56,740 --> 00:01:06,240
Icebergs are born when they calve off of glaciers or break off of ice shelves.

11
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:10,420
Each iceberg has its own individual personality.

12
00:01:10,420 --> 00:01:16,900
They have a distinct way of interacting with their environment and their experiences.

13
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:22,780
Some refuse to give up and hold on to the bitter end, while others can't take it anymore

14
00:01:22,780 --> 00:01:27,580
and crumble in a fit of dramatic passion.

15
00:01:27,580 --> 00:01:31,780
It's easy to think when you look at an iceberg that they're isolated, that they're separate

16
00:01:31,780 --> 00:01:33,620
and alone.

17
00:01:33,620 --> 00:01:39,220
Much like we as humans sometimes view ourselves, but the reality is far from it.

18
00:01:39,220 --> 00:01:45,460
As an iceberg melts, I am breathing in its ancient atmosphere.

19
00:01:45,460 --> 00:01:55,460
As the iceberg melts, it is releasing mineral-rich fresh water that nourishes many forms of life.

20
00:01:55,460 --> 00:02:00,900
I approached photographing these icebergs as if I'm making portraits of my ancestors,

21
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:06,480
knowing that in these individual moments, they exist in that way and will never exist

22
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:08,140
that way again.

23
00:02:08,140 --> 00:02:10,820
It is not a death when they melt.

24
00:02:10,820 --> 00:02:22,060
It is not an end, but a continuation of their path through the cycle of life.

25
00:02:22,060 --> 00:02:28,140
Some of the ice in the icebergs that I photograph is very young, a couple thousand years old,

26
00:02:28,140 --> 00:02:42,380
and some of the ice is over a hundred thousand years old.

27
00:02:42,380 --> 00:02:47,740
The last pictures I'd like to show you are of an iceberg that I photographed in Kekataswak,

28
00:02:47,740 --> 00:02:48,940
Greenland.

29
00:02:48,940 --> 00:02:55,380
It's a very rare occasion that you get to actually witness an iceberg rolling.

30
00:02:55,380 --> 00:02:56,780
So here it is.

31
00:02:56,780 --> 00:03:02,780
You can see on the left side a small boat that's about a 15-foot boat.

32
00:03:02,780 --> 00:03:09,780
And I'd like you to pay attention to the shape of the iceberg and where it is at the waterline.

33
00:03:09,780 --> 00:03:13,420
You can see here it begins to roll and the boat has moved to the other side and the man

34
00:03:13,420 --> 00:03:14,520
is standing there.

35
00:03:14,520 --> 00:03:17,040
This is an average-sized Greenlandic iceberg.

36
00:03:17,040 --> 00:03:21,940
It's about 120 feet above the water, or 40 meters.

37
00:03:21,940 --> 00:03:23,540
This video is real time.

38
00:03:51,940 --> 00:03:59,520
And, just like that, the iceberg shows you a different side of its personality.

39
00:03:59,520 --> 00:03:59,920
Thank you.

